explain.narrative:
## Core concept
EXISTS tests whether a subquery returns at least one row. NOT EXISTS tests whether it returns no rows. The subquery is commonly correlated to the current outer row, and the values selected inside it do not determine the truth of EXISTS.

## Syntax
```sql
SELECT columns
FROM outer_table AS o
WHERE EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM related_table AS r
  WHERE r.key = o.key
);
```

## SQLite sample database
The following examples use a small fictional dataset. Each worked example starts with a repeatable setup script so it can be run independently in the SQL Playground.

## Worked examples
### 1. Find customers who have at least one order
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,150);
SELECT c.customer_name FROM customers AS c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders AS o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id) ORDER BY c.customer_id;
```
Expected result: Asha; Chitra

The correlated subquery checks for at least one matching order for each customer.

### 2. Find customers with no orders
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,150);
SELECT c.customer_name FROM customers AS c WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders AS o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id) ORDER BY c.customer_id;
```
Expected result: Bilal; Dev

NOT EXISTS is true when the correlated subquery finds no matching order.

### 3. Find customers with an order above a threshold
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,150);
SELECT c.customer_name FROM customers AS c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders AS o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id AND o.amount > 100) ORDER BY c.customer_id;
```
Expected result: Chitra

The amount condition is evaluated inside the existence test.

### 4. Use EXISTS with an additional outer filter
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,150);
SELECT c.customer_name FROM customers AS c WHERE c.city = 'Rajahmundry' AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders AS o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id) ORDER BY c.customer_id;
```
Expected result: Asha

The outer city condition and correlated existence condition must both be true.

### 5. Return each qualifying customer once
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER NOT NULL, amount NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,150);
SELECT c.customer_id, c.customer_name FROM customers AS c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders AS o WHERE o.customer_id = c.customer_id) ORDER BY c.customer_id;
```
Expected result: 1 | Asha; 3 | Chitra

EXISTS filters the customer rows; it does not multiply them when a customer has multiple matching orders.

## Query behaviour and dialect notes
The examples target SQLite and use portable core SQL syntax. Column positions and compatible data types must align across set-operation branches. Result order is not guaranteed without an outer ORDER BY.

## Common mistakes
- Writing an uncorrelated subquery when the requirement is per outer row.
- Using NOT IN without considering NULL values.
- Assuming EXISTS returns the matching child rows; it filters outer rows.
- Joining child rows when only existence is required and then needing DISTINCT.
- Forgetting that NOT EXISTS means no matching row exists.

## Best practices
Use explicit aliases and readable predicates. Test edge cases such as missing matches, duplicate values, empty sets and NULLs. Keep the database dialect visible whenever a feature is not portable.

